Beryllium: the GPU process, the network service and the storage service cut what they send a confined renderer

Purpose: plan/18 section 1 (feature matrix S-IPC-01; adversarial review
  plan/16, finding BER-05; README "Still to finish", item 1). The browser
  cuts every descriptor it sends a confined renderer
  (beryllium-sender-side-rights.patch). A renderer also holds direct links
  to other children: in the survey of 10 November 2026 one renderer held two
  to the GPU process, one to the network service and one to the storage
  service. What travels on those links was limited by the receiving
  renderer only, which a compromised renderer need not do.
  A child does not know who is at the other end of a link: the browser makes
  the link and hands each child one end. So the browser, which knows which
  of its children are confined, says so on the link.
  - mojo/core/ipcz_driver/transport.h: a member, beryllium_peer_confined_.
  - mojo/core/ipcz_driver/driver.cc, CreateTransports(): when the browser
    makes a link between children A and B, the end B will hold is marked if
    A is a registered confined peer, and the end A will hold if B is.
  - mojo/core/ipcz_driver/transport.cc: the mark travels in TransportHeader,
    in the byte that was padding, so the header keeps its size. Serialize()
    writes it, Deserialize() reads it, and Transmit() cuts every descriptor
    sent over a marked transport, as it already did on the browser's own
    transports to confined renderers.
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: LimitSentDescriptor() accepts a peer
    pid of 0, for a sender that knows the peer is confined and not which
    process it is. Its log line then names no pid; the process that wrote
    the line is the sender.
  Who may set the mark: a set mark means "cut what you send", so it is
    believed from anyone, and a forged one makes a sender cut more than it
    had to. A clear mark is believed only from a source the receiving end
    trusts, which for a child is its broker, the browser. A transport that
    arrives from any other source while the lockdown is on is treated as
    marked.
  What changes for a renderer: nothing it could do before. Descriptors that
    used to arrive whole and be cut by the renderer now arrive cut; the
    policy per kind is the same one. With the lockdown off (the default) no
    link is marked and nothing changes.
  The wire format changes within one build only: every process is the same
    binary. A helper binary from another build must not be mixed in.
Not covered here: descriptors that do not travel over a Mojo transport
  (plan/18 section 1.5: the list of sendmsg calls with SCM_RIGHTS in the
  tree as built), and the committed table of which process types may hold a
  link to a renderer.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-descriptor-rights.patch
  and beryllium-sender-side-rights.patch, whose Transmit() hunk this
  extends.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.21).
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py test_lockdown_log
  counts the cut lines by the type of the process that wrote them (the pid
  at the start of each log line, against the browser's process list) and
  notes them; class SenderAlone requires the policy on every descriptor of
  every renderer with the receiver's limits off, which holds only if every
  sender cuts.
security-review: required (IPC boundary, descriptor rights)

